    excerpt: "The SEC cleared eleven spot Bitcoin ETFs for trading, ending years of denials and unlocking the most consequential structural change in Bitcoin's market history.",
    content: `<p>The U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission cleared eleven spot Bitcoin exchange-traded funds in a single block order late Wednesday, ending a decade-long contest that began with the Winklevoss twins' 2013 application and ran through more than twenty rejected filings before yesterday. Trading on products from BlackRock, Fidelity, Ark/21Shares, Bitwise, Invesco, VanEck, Valkyrie, Franklin Templeton, WisdomTree and a converted Grayscale Bitcoin Trust opens at the New York Stock Exchange and Nasdaq tomorrow morning.</p><p>The decision was reached only after the U.S. Court of Appeals for the D.C. Circuit ruled in August that the SEC's prior denials were arbitrary and capricious, faulting the agency for failing to explain why a futures-based Bitcoin ETF posed materially different risks than a spot product. Chair Gary Gensler, in a statement issued alongside the approvals, stressed that the order applies narrowly to a single non-security commodity ETP and emphasized that the agency was not endorsing Bitcoin or the broader crypto industry. Two commissioners dissented in writing.</p><p>Issuers had spent the preceding weeks racing on fee compression. Bitwise, Ark/21Shares and Fidelity will list with expense ratios at or below twenty-five basis points. Several products have waived management fees outright for the first six months or until cumulative assets under management cross one billion dollars. Grayscale, by contrast, kept its converted GBTC headline fee at 1.50 percent, an open invitation for the more than twenty-five billion dollars of locked-up trust assets to migrate into cheaper structures.</p><p>Buy-side reaction has been measured but firm. Allocators at three large U.S. wealth platforms told reporters they had model-portfolio drafts ready to integrate the new tickers, with most pencilling in single-digit basis-point allocations across diversified mandates. Coinbase Custody, which is named as the qualified custodian on most of the eleven products, will see a step-change in institutionally held coin balances. The internal modeling at one Tier-1 issuer pegs combined first-year inflows at thirty to fifty billion dollars; rival shops at the lower end of that range still describe the product as the most consequential ETF launch since the original gold trusts.</p><p>The structural significance for Bitcoin itself is harder to overstate. The asset has spent fifteen years walled off from the largest pools of U.S. retirement and advisory capital because the only available regulated wrappers were a futures-based ETF with persistent roll cost and a trust trading at deep discount to its underlying coins. Tomorrow, an investor with a brokerage account can buy a basket of physically backed Bitcoin in the same workflow as a Treasury fund, and a registered investment adviser can recommend it without operational gymnastics around custody.</p><p>The next data points come quickly. Day-one volume will be parsed for evidence of pent-up retail interest; cumulative inflows into the BlackRock and Fidelity products will be watched against GBTC outflows to gauge whether the launch prints a clean institutional bid or simply rotates existing trust holders into cheaper sleeves. Issuers expect price discovery to remain offshore for the first weeks while regulated arbitrage desks build out their basket-trading infrastructure. The bigger test arrives in March, when several large RIA platforms will decide whether to add the products to their pre-approved fund lists.</p>`,
