    slug: "bitcoin-crosses-100000-for-the-first-time",
    title: "Bitcoin Crosses $100,000 for the First Time",
    excerpt: "The round-number milestone fell in a single overnight session, less than two months after the U.S. election results re-priced the macro and regulatory backdrop.",
    content: `<p>Bitcoin printed a price beginning with six digits for the first time in its sixteen-year history, briefly tagging $103,800 in offshore venues at 02:14 GMT before settling just above the $100,000 mark on regulated U.S. exchanges. The breakout closed out a five-week stretch in which spot Bitcoin ETFs absorbed multiples of network issuance and corporate treasuries quietly added to existing stacks, and which began with the November 5 election result re-pricing both the macro and regulatory backdrop for the asset.</p><p>The catalysts are by now familiar. The U.S. presidential election produced an administration explicitly friendlier to crypto, with the president-elect publicly nominating a pro-industry SEC chair candidate and floating the idea of a strategic Bitcoin reserve at the federal level. Spot ETF demand has continued to absorb roughly two times daily issuance through November, with BlackRock's IBIT alone now holding over half a million coins. Long-term holders, whose behaviour Glassnode tracks via the supply held by addresses that have not moved coins in over twelve months, have been quietly accumulating rather than distributing into the strength.</p><p>Volatility profile through the move has been notably contained. Implied vol on listed BTC options at Deribit is sitting in the mid-fifties, well below the twelve-month average and dramatically below the realized vol of past cycle-peak runs. The realized vol on perpetual futures at the major offshore venues is at multi-year lows. Open interest in the U.S. CME Bitcoin futures complex, by contrast, has climbed steadily and now sits above $30 billion in notional, the largest figure in the venue's history and a clean indicator that institutional positioning is real rather than a leverage-driven retail wave.</p><p>Buy-side reaction has been measured. Strategists at three large U.S. wealth platforms reiterated their existing single-digit basis-point allocation guidance to advisers, with most arguing that the macro and structural backdrop justify holding existing positions through the round-number print rather than chasing fresh exposure. Macro funds with public allocations — Pantera, Blockstone, Grayscale's institutional arm — have begun to publicly discuss Bitcoin in the same multi-asset framing they use for gold, copper and oil. The framing matters because it changes how Bitcoin is sized inside diversified mandates, generally for the better.</p><p>For the broader market, the round number is psychological more than structural. On-chain flows had been pricing a $100,000 print for weeks, and the cohort of holders who have been waiting for that level to take profit is now reasonably well documented in the on-chain data. Market-makers report that the depth of bid-side interest at $95,000 to $98,000 has been heavy enough that intraday pullbacks have not extended through that band, suggesting the floor of acceptance for the new range is close at hand. The historical pattern, where round numbers act as multi-week consolidation magnets, is the most likely path forward.</p><p>The next catalysts to watch are inauguration-week regulatory signals — a confirmed pro-industry SEC chair, an executive order on crypto custody or banking, or movement on the strategic-reserve concept could each extend the structural bid — and the January CME futures roll, where institutional positioning will be most visible. Year-end seasonality also tends to thin out market depth, which can produce outsized moves in either direction on relatively modest order flow. Most trading desks expect the $100,000 level to remain the anchor of the new range through the holiday window, with the next major test of the trend coming with the January ETF flow data.</p>`,
